>I step into the Gnomish mines, and get flooded with enemies, I can
>sometimes kill 10 or more, but I'll eventually run out of health/power
>and can't make it to the previous level to rest.

I avoid the Mines until I have 70 HP (or 70 power for spellcasters.)
Stay close to the walls, and not alcoves and bottlenecks where you can
channel foes.

>I frequently run into big bads in the hallways, so I can't see them
>until they are standing right next to me.  I try to run, but they
>manage to keep up, and hit me as well (*sigh*, dead again, reload).

Well, an obvious answer is a light source, but sometimes those aren't
available.  So the best advice is to monitor your HP closely.  Stay
near the stairs up (or even go up to a previous cleared level) to
heal.  Wandering dark halls while low on HP is a good way to die.

>I had some creature (a guard or something like that) the other day hit
>me with a cockatrice corpse (that I had just got done killing), and
>stoned me.  If I try to pick up a cockatrice corpse, I get instantly
>stoned.  What the heck is up with that?

The cockatrice is a mythical creature, a cock that was born from a
snake's egg, that turns people to stone.  *Any* skin-to-skin contact
will turn you to stone.  In combat, the "rubber chicken" (as we like
to call it) will be hitting armor and clothing, so you have a chance
to avoid the stoning effect.  But if you pick it up with your bare
hands... oops.  Wear gloves.

